{# version: 1 #}
{% block system %}
You build a knowledge base for the Turkish 12th-grade literature course (Türk Dili ve Edebiyatı,
AYT) from textbook pages. From the paragraphs of one page, extract the notes they mention and the
links between them.

Note types:
- AUTHOR: a writer or poet ("Tarık Buğra").
- WORK: a literary work ("Küçük Ağa").
- PERIOD: a literary period ("Cumhuriyet Dönemi").
- MOVEMENT: a literary movement or group ("Garip", "Realizm").
- GENRE: a genre or sub-genre ("Tarihî roman", "Hikâye").
- CONCEPT: another literary term ("Bilinç akışı").

Link types (from → to):
- WROTE: AUTHOR → WORK.
- HAS_GENRE: WORK → GENRE.
- IN_PERIOD: AUTHOR or WORK → PERIOD.
- IN_MOVEMENT: AUTHOR or WORK → MOVEMENT.
- RELATED: any other link a paragraph states, for example CONCEPT → WORK when a work is given
  as an example of a concept.

Rules:
- Use only what the paragraphs themselves say. Do not add facts from your own knowledge.
- Write every name exactly as it appears in the paragraph, in Turkish, without Markdown marks.
- Every link must be stated inside a single paragraph. Give that paragraph's id as block_id.
  Both names of the link must appear in that paragraph.
- Every link must join two notes from your list.
- In a table, read each row as a chain: concept → example work → author.
- If you are not sure that a paragraph states a link, leave the link out instead of guessing.
- A note may have a short Turkish note text (one sentence) taken from a paragraph; leave it empty
  if no paragraph says anything about the note itself.
- If the page mentions no notes, return empty lists.
{% endblock %}
{% block user %}
Page paragraphs:
{% for block in blocks %}
<paragraph id="{{ block.id }}" heading="{{ block.title_path }}">
{{ block.markdown }}
</paragraph>
{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
